WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.547 --> 00:00:10.853
(people shouting)

00:00:10.853 --> 00:00:12.700
- I was shocked.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.810
There is this guy lying on the ground

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.090
and he is not a danger to anyone.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.930
He's just lying there

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.110
and two or three officers
are beating him with batons

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.020
and everybody's just standing around them.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.253
Nothing is happening.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.810
I mean, this was, for me,
the paradigmatic bad action.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.140
How can you do that?

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.490
- [Narrator] It's a question at the heart

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:39.640
of many alleged police
misconduct incidents,

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.260
from the beating of Rodney King in 1991

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.930
to the choke-hold death
of Eric Garner in 2014,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.920
and the killing of George Floyd in 2020.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.807
Why did other officers not intervene?

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:53.050
- Back off.
- He's not responding.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.400
- The ideal is really simple.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.880
You stand up

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.690
and do something and sometimes
something very limited

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.270
to stop a fellow officer

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.823
from doing unnecessary harm to someone.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.420
- [Narrator] Ervin Staub is a psychologist

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.580
who has studied the concept
of active bystandership.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.200
He first applied it to policing

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.853
after the Rodney Kind beating.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.820
Now it's the foundation of a new version

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.050
of active bystander police training,

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.510
created by Georgetown
University Law School's

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.460
Innovative Policing Program

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.910
called Active Bystandership
for Law Enforcement, or ABLE.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.200
- What brings us here
today? The ABLE training.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.700
- [Narrator] Since the
murder of George Floyd

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.040
during an arrest by Minneapolis police,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.970
over 100 law enforcement agencies

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.410
across the US have signed
on for the training.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:48.310
Teaching police officers to
intervene against one another

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.340
is a complicated mission.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.730
- The tradition is that police officers

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.090
are supposed to help each other

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:58.120
and support each other no matter what.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.750
So there is a very profound need

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.240
to change their culture.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.220
- [Presenter] So we may
condense a little bit.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.240
- It's family I guess
it's the simplest way

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.155
of putting it.

00:02:07.155 --> 00:02:10.540
It is being an extended family.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.460
There's loyalty to my squad,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.223
loyalty to my subordinates, my detectives.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.570
- One of the themes I've
already heard so far

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.490
this morning is loyalty.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.930
We talked about when
the call comes, we go,

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:22.763
there's no questions.

00:02:22.763 --> 00:02:25.150
- [Narrator] At a recent
training in New Jersey

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.050
for law enforcement
agencies across the state,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.290
officers began tackling this challenge.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.550
- We are loyal to one another.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.100
You've got to be wiling

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.850
to have a hard conversation with somebody.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.310
If you're loyal to them,

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.634
if you really care about them,

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:44.920
you are gonna have that integrity

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.383
and not just have that blind allegiance.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.150
- [Officer] I think you need to be humble.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:51.794
I think you need to learn.

00:02:51.794 --> 00:02:53.900
I think the biggest problem
that we have right now

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.950
in all this shit that's been going on

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.110
is piss poor police training.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.390
That's what it comes down to.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.590
- [Narrator] The ABLE program seeks

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.210
to go beyond previous versions

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.140
of active bystander police training

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.190
by teaching officers

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:09.263
how to overcome the psychological
hurdles to intervening.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.400
It's rooted in Staub's experience

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.406
as a child in the Holocaust.

00:03:14.406 --> 00:03:17.500
(dramatic music)

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.880
- I was a six-year-old child in Hungary

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:26.880
at the time when about
450,000 Hungarian Jews

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.960
were pushed into train, cars,

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.963
taken to Auschwitz and most
of them immediately killed.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:42.590
This woman who was taking care of us,

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:46.000
took me and my sister into hiding.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.740
One thing that I learned

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:52.370
from life and also from research,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.680
bystanders can be very powerful,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.360
influencing other bystanders

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.713
and their behavior.

00:04:00.954 --> 00:04:02.830
- Good morning, guys.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.010
Can we line up for inspection?

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.720
- [Narrator] Sergeant
Anthony Lynn has been

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.330
with the Voorhees Township
New Jersey Police Department

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.893
for nine years.

00:04:10.893 --> 00:04:12.717
- You wanna turn to the right.

00:04:12.717 --> 00:04:14.330
(car indictor clicking)

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.930
- [Narrator] He and his
fellow officers recently

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.163
went through active bystander training.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.640
- Everybody's human,
everybody has their moments

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.100
and sometimes you just need

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.280
to be reeled back a little bit

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.120
so that you understand
that it's not a situation

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.120
where you wanna be a snitch

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.250
or be labeled as a snitch

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.120
but you wanna make sure
that they get home safe,

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.450
we get home safe.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.200
(dramatic music)

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.880
- If we've been honest with each other,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.130
we don't like changing
law enforcement, right?

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.650
- [Narrator] The training seeks
to address cultural change

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.570
in a few key ways.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.410
The leaders and chiefs of
agencies are trained first

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:59.113
and instructors are members
of the departments they train.

00:04:59.113 --> 00:05:00.660
- Get the class to talk about it,

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.135
get the role players to talk about it.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:03.499
How did you feel about that?

00:05:03.499 --> 00:05:05.477
What tactics did you use?

00:05:05.477 --> 00:05:07.170
- [Narrator] The
participants in this class

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.520
will train officers in their
respective departments.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.613
- There's some officers
that they would interpret

00:05:12.613 --> 00:05:14.277
that intervention as saying,

00:05:14.277 --> 00:05:16.980
"You're incompetent, you can't handle it."

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.210
And you know, you need to
have a cultural component

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.703
that says, you know, that's okay.

00:05:20.703 --> 00:05:22.653
It's okay to have somebody step in.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:28.250
- We also train people to
accept the intervention.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:33.070
So you are learning how to intervene,

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.560
first, in as limited way as possible.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.520
And escalate as necessary

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.590
and how to accept training.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.490
- It's a hot call, so a
lot of cops are showing.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:45.410
Early in my career,

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:47.540
there was an occasion where we had arrest

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.060
and I'm escorting him back to one

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.770
of the patrol cars to transfer
him back to headquarters.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.880
One of the senior
officers at the time kind

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.640
of stepped and grabbed
him, opened the door

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.650
and seemed like about to
basically slam the guy

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:00.770
in the door there.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:02.440
Thankfully a senior officer was there

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.460
and kind of intervened and stepped in

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.750
but as I think back, I
should have said something

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:07.583
at that time.

00:06:07.583 --> 00:06:08.690
It shouldn't had to take someone else

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:11.350
to step in to intervene.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:12.750
(dramatic music)

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.580
- [Narrator] The program
also teaches officers

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.450
to look for allies when intervening.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.477
- And you say to a fellow officer,

00:06:19.477 --> 00:06:22.160
"This is bad, we should do something."

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.900
And then you jointly take action.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.030
- [Narrator] The creators
of the ABLE program plan

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.360
to study data from participating agencies

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.890
to evaluate its effectiveness.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.130
The impact of other versions
of active bystander training

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.090
has been inconclusive.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.900
The New Orleans Police
Department reported a decrease

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.240
in civilian complaints

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.160
after participating in a previous version

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.500
of the training but those
results may have been influenced

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.843
by other reforms mandated by
the Department of Justice.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.410
To have an impact, the challenge for Staub

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.060
and the ABLE program

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.290
is not just to show how to intervene

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.693
but to make it acceptable to intervene.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.140
- We have the ability to shift the culture

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:10.140
within ourselves and we have
the ability to repair the trust

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:12.500
with the community.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.300
We have to be able to protect ourselves

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.130
and sometimes pull somebody back

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.620
from potentially harming someone else

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.920
or harming themselves.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:22.960
It seems to me like it's going

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.060
to be a very important tool going forward.

